# .gitignore — InsureFlow DataPipeline
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Built from what THIS repo actually generates/consumes — see comments below
# for why each block exists. When in doubt about a new file: if it contains
# a private key, a passphrase, a token, or is generated by a tool run, ignore
# it. If it's a PUBLIC key or a fingerprint, it's meant to be committed.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Secrets: env files ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# pipeline/config.py (PipelineSettings) loads from .env via pydantic-settings.
# entrypoint.sh's dev-mode path also expects a local .env. NEVER commit one
# with real values — only .env.example (tracked) should ever be in git.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ── Secrets: PGP keys ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# generate_pgp_keypair.py writes PRIVATE keys to /tmp/ by default (outside the
# repo), but if --output-dir is ever pointed inside the repo, or a private key
# is copied here by mistake, these patterns catch it. PUBLIC keys and
# fingerprint files are intentionally NOT ignored — they're meant to be
# committed (see generate_pgp_keypair.py's own "safe to commit" messaging).
keys/*_private_key.asc
keys/private_key.asc
keys/*.priv
*.priv.asc
# Explicitly un-ignore what generate_pgp_keypair.py says is safe to commit:
!keys/*_public_key.asc
!keys/public_key.asc
!keys/*_fingerprint.txt
!keys/fingerprint.txt

# ── Secrets: SSH / SFTP keys ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# sftp_client.py reads SFTP_SSH_KEY_PATH (default /run/secrets/sftp_rsa_key,
# outside the repo) but local dev setups sometimes drop a copy in-repo.
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa
id_rsa.*
known_hosts
sftp_rsa_key*

# ── Secrets: GCP service account keys ──────────────────────────────────────────
# config.py's GCS_SA_KEY_PATH and docker/entrypoint.sh's GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS
# can point at a local JSON key file for dev. CI writes its own to /tmp/sa.json
# (outside the repo), but guard against a local copy here too.
*-sa-key.json
*-service-account*.json
sa-key.json
gcp-credentials.json

# ── Terraform ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# State lives remotely in the GCS backend (terraform/providers.tf), but the
# LOCAL working files below are still created on every `terraform init/plan/apply`
# and must never be committed — .terraform/ can itself contain cached provider
# credentials, and *.tfstate can contain secret values in plain text.
**/.terraform/*
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfstate.backup
crash.log
crash.*.log
*.tfplan
*.tfvars.json
# terraform.tfvars files ARE committed in this repo (environments/dev and
# environments/prod) — they contain no secrets, only project IDs and sizing.
# Do NOT blanket-ignore *.tfvars here; only ignore *.tfvars.json (override files).
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json
.terraformrc
terraform.rc

# ── Python ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/

# ── Test / coverage artifacts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# requirements.txt pins pytest-cov; .gitlab-ci.yml writes coverage.xml as a
# CI artifact — that's fine for the CI job's own artifact upload, but it
# should never be committed to the repo itself.
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
.tox/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# ── Airflow local run artifacts ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# docker-compose.yml mounts airflow-logs as a named volume, but local
# non-containerized runs (e.g. `airflow standalone` for quick DAG testing)
# write these directly into the project directory.
logs/
airflow.db
airflow.cfg
standalone_admin_password.txt
webserver_config.py

# ── Docker ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.env.docker

# ── OS / editor cruft ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~

# ── Generated runtime secrets file ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# docker/entrypoint.sh writes this to /tmp/ at container startup (outside any
# mounted repo path), but ignore defensively in case it's ever relocated.
runtime_secrets.env